 "cards": [
  {
   "key": "whats_going_on",
   "title": "What is going on in the body",
   "text": "Hurler syndrome is caused by changes in a gene called IDUA, which is like an instruction page for making one particular helper molecule [Ede703e7, Ea3d2556]. That helper molecule is an enzyme, a tool that breaks things apart, called alpha-L-iduronidase [Ee43cb3f, E9977f00, E12070de, E9ae13b7]. When this enzyme is missing or not working, the body cannot break down certain long sugar chains the way it normally would [E834d07e, E60f9b02, E99c17bc]. This happens inside tiny clean-up parts of cells called lysosomes [Ee9d9d1e, E4f69a3f, Ebb30cb1]. Because the sugar chains cannot be broken down, one kind called dermatan sulfate builds up in tissues [Ef4f70c3, E7ef79ca, Ef5b2735, E44b1b56]. A second kind called heparan sulfate builds up too [Ea90608d, E9add978, Ecc81260]. A paper reports that this buildup can involve the heart [Ea785b30]. A paper also reports fewer of certain inner-ear cells compared with people who do not have Hurler syndrome [Ea240b4a].",
   "citations": [
    "Ede703e7",
    "Ea3d2556",
    "Ee43cb3f",
    "E9977f00",
    "E12070de",
