  {
   "key": "similar",
   "title": "Diseases that share the same biology",
   "text": "MPS II, also called Hunter syndrome, is biologically similar because a paper reports it is also caused by a missing enzyme inside the lysosome, the cell's clean-up part, just like Hurler syndrome [E834d07e, Ef5d5a50, Efa51226, E5228a4d]. Both conditions involve a buildup of dermatan sulfate, a long sugar chain that cannot be broken down properly [Ef4f70c3, E385b672, E24fd8ba]. Both also involve a buildup of heparan sulfate [Ea90608d, E1f3fa46, E873fb62]. According to HPO, children with either MPS I or MPS II can show extra sugar chains in their urine and can have an umbilical hernia, a small bulge near the belly button [E8e6e9a9, Ed232c04, Eac52a80, E38be86b]. The four Sanfilippo syndromes, MPS IIIA, IIIB, IIIC, and IIID, are also biologically similar to Hurler syndrome because papers report they share the same pattern of a missing lysosomal enzyme and a buildup of heparan sulfate, even though the exact enzyme involved is different in each one [Ec586fdd, E8282821, Eae4e3d8, E8a7d8c7, E7373ae4, E108a4c3, E9f300a9, Ef8f5811].",
