  {
   "key": "what_exists",
   "title": "Groups, studies, and research that exist",
   "text": "Several patient groups focus on MPS I (Hurler), including the MPS Society in the United Kingdom, the Canadian Society for Mucopolysaccharide & Related Diseases, AIMPS ETS in Italy, the Canadian MPS Society, MPS Austria, MPS-Deutschland, and the National MPS Society in the United States [E7291dc8, E49f99f7, E68fcb3d, Ec766e13, E0aadd6b, E0537b14, Ec9d5c8a]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, several studies are linked to MPS I (Hurler), including a Phase I/II trial of JR-446 recruiting in Germany, the United Kingdom, and the United States, a Phase I/II study of JR-441 in Germany, and a long-term follow-up study of people who received the gene therapies SB-318, SB-913, or SB-FIX in the United States, though that one has ended [E5b80b7a, Eaeeff97, E811d9f2]. According to ClinicalTrials.gov, none of these studies currently list a site in your country [E5b80b7a, Eaeeff97, E811d9f2]. NIH NINDS and NIH NIGMS are listed as funders of research grants connected to MPS I (Hurler) [Ee3fd178, E3eecbab, E1701055, Ec27516a, E941b226, Efec0abc]. For the related disease MPS II (Hunter syndrome), patient groups Project Alive and The MPS II Research Fund exist, and according to ClinicalTrials.gov, industry-run studies include a Phase III study of JR-141 and a study comparing tividenofusp alfa with idursulfase, run by Denali Therapeutics [Eb0160a0, Eea9b26d, Ec92098b]. For MPS IIIA (Sanfilippo syndrome type A), patient groups such as the Cure Sanfilippo Foundation, Team Sanfilippo Foundation, Fundación Stop Sanfilippo, Stichting Sanfilippo Fonds, Sanfilippo Children's Foundation, and Asociación MPS-LISOSOMALES España exist, and according to ClinicalTrials.gov a gene transfer study is recruiting in Australia, Spain, and the United States [E80ea2ad, E2fbfe1f, E211dd58, E1f2ae8c, Eb6b5ea1, E9d0375e].",
